## What is this

A **x86_64 kernel built from scratch**, in NASM, with no framework and no tutorial-following on autopilot. The goal isn't to ship a usable OS, it's to actually understand what happens between power-on and a scheduler running processes: real mode, the transition to protected mode, then long mode, paging, interrupts, and everything else the CPU hides behind abstractions the rest of the time.

This is a learning and portfolio project. Expect rough edges, and expect the boot chain to be more polished than the parts further out.

## 🧭 Roadmap

Progress is tracked through [GitHub milestones](https://github.com/mitsuakki/baremetal-x86_64-kernel/milestones), from real mode up to userspace:

| Milestone | Focus |
|---|---|
| **[M1 - Boot to long mode](https://github.com/mitsuakki/baremetal-x86_64-kernel/milestone/1)** | real mode → protected mode → long mode, minimal paging to enable it, text output working |
| **[M2 - Interrupts](https://github.com/mitsuakki/baremetal-x86_64-kernel/milestone/2)** | IDT, exception handlers, PIC/APIC init |
| **[M3 - Paging](https://github.com/mitsuakki/baremetal-x86_64-kernel/milestone/3)** | proper page tables, higher-half kernel jump, dynamic mapping |
| **[M4 - Timer](https://github.com/mitsuakki/baremetal-x86_64-kernel/milestone/4)** | APIC timer or PIT, tick counter |
| **[M5 - Memory management](https://github.com/mitsuakki/baremetal-x86_64-kernel/milestone/5)** | physical frame allocator, kernel heap |
| **[M6 - Scheduling](https://github.com/mitsuakki/baremetal-x86_64-kernel/milestone/6)** | context switch, cooperative round-robin |
| **[M7 - Syscalls](https://github.com/mitsuakki/baremetal-x86_64-kernel/milestone/7)** | syscall/sysret, first userspace binary |

## 🛠️ Building & running

```bash
git clone https://github.com/mitsuakki/baremetal-x86_64-kernel.git
cd baremetal-x86_64-kernel
make
qemu-system-x86_64 -kernel build/kernel.bin
```

*(adjust the exact `make`/`qemu` invocation to match your actual build setup)*

## 📚 Notes

Built with NASM and a lot of Intel/AMD manual reading. If you're going through the same rabbit hole, [OSDev Wiki](https://wiki.osdev.org/) is where most of this started.

